  "explanation_methodology": "Global importance uses TreeExplainer SHAP values computed with the interventional feature-perturbation strategy against a fixed, deterministic background of 100 labelled training rows. Per sample and per original feature, the SHAP values of that feature's transformed columns (one-hot groups summed) are aggregated into one net contribution; global importance is the mean of the absolute net contributions over the report sample. SHAP values are feature-attribution values describing the model's output: how much each feature moves this model's P(anomaly) for a given record away from the baseline (the model's mean P(anomaly) over the background). They are NOT causal explanations of network behaviour.",
  "limitations": [
    "SHAP attributions explain the MODEL's output, not the underlying network phenomenon: they are not causal explanations.",
    "Metrics and attributions come from the labelled training distribution only (Train_data.csv); the unlabelled Test_data.csv is never scored or explained.",
    "The interventional baseline depends on the 100-row background sample; a different background changes individual attributions (the baseline is the model's mean P(anomaly) over that sample).",
    "TreeExplainer attributions are exact for the tree ensemble given the background, but they describe a surrogate view of feature effects; correlated features split credit between them.",
    "Global importance is a mean over the report sample; rare attack patterns may be under-represented and their drivers under-weighted.",
    "Per-sample contributions are about the trained model as-is; they do not measure what would happen if the feature were different in the world (no intervention was performed on the network)."
  ],
